<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · 1788 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Auld Lang Syne</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Scottish song by Robert Burns from 1788, sung at midnight on New Year's Eve to remember old friends.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Robert Burns (from an older Scottish folk song)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Scots</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Scots</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Scotland, United Kingdom</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1788 (set to its present tune 1799)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Robert Burns (1788, from an older song; he died in 1796))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Auld Lang Syne&quot; is a poem in Scots that Robert Burns wrote in 1788 on the basis of an older Scottish folk song. Burns sent it to the Scots Musical Museum that year, describing it as an old song that had never been printed or written down until he took it down from an old man. Part of it was indeed collected rather than composed: a ballad &quot;Old Long Syne&quot;, printed by James Watson in 1711, is close to Burns's first verse and chorus, and both probably come from the same old song. The rest is usually attributed to Burns himself. The title phrase, roughly &quot;old long since&quot; or &quot;days gone by&quot;, also appears in poems by Robert Ayton and Allan Ramsay.</p><p>The words first had another tune, traceable to about 1700, which Burns thought mediocre. The melody sung today was first printed in 1799 in George Thomson's Select <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, which also moved a verse to its present place. It is a pentatonic Scots folk tune, probably first a quick dance; it is not certain that Burns meant his words for it. William Shield seems to quote it in his opera Rosina (1782), perhaps from a common source such as the strathspey &quot;The Miller's Wedding&quot;. Haydn arranged the song for Thomson's collections, and Beethoven published an arrangement in 1814.</p><p>Singing it at Hogmanay, the Scottish New Year, quickly became a custom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>, and emigrants from Scotland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Ireland carried it around the world. In the Scottish custom the singers stand in a circle holding hands and cross their arms only at the start of the last verse; crossing hands at parting arose in the mid-19th century among Freemasons and other fraternal societies. Guy Lombardo's band played it on New Year's Eve from 1929 for decades, and his 1947 recording is still played in Times Square after the ball drops.</p><p>The song became a general song of endings and farewells: at funerals, graduations, Burns suppers, the close of Scout jamborees and the passing-out parades of military academies across the Commonwealth. It was played at British handovers of power, among them the departure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>'s last British governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chris-patten">Chris Patten</a>, on 30 June 1997. Its pentatonic tune suited musical traditions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>: it is the melody of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s &quot;Hotaru no Hikari&quot;, sung at graduations, and of a well-known song by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>. From 1919 to 1945 it carried the anthem of the Korean exile government, from 1945 to 1948 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>'s national anthem was sung to it, and the Maldives used it for their anthem until 1972.</p><p>The first recording was made on a wax cylinder in 1898 by Charles Samuel Myers and Alfred Cort Haddon during an expedition to record the music of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torres-strait-islanders">Torres Strait Islanders</a>. Members of the European Parliament sang it in January 2020 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brexit">Brexit</a> withdrawal agreement was passed.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of friendship and memory: it asks whether old friends and old times should be forgotten, and answers by calling for them to be remembered together. The singers share a drink and recall a shared youth that has since been divided by distance, then join hands. The mood is warm, a little wistful, and festive.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Scots</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Scots</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">Should auld acquaintance be forgot,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Should old acquaintance be forgotten,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">And never brought to mind?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And never brought to mind?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">Should auld acquaintance be forgot,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Should old acquaintance be forgotten,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">And days o' lang syne?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And the days of long ago?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">For auld lang syne, my dear,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For old times long ago, my dear,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">For auld lang syne,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For old times long ago,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">We'll tak' a cup o' kindness yet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We'll take a cup of kindness yet,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">For auld lang syne.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For old times long ago.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">We twa hae run about the braes,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We two have run about the hillsides,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">And pu'd the gowans fine;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And pulled the pretty daisies;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">But we've wander'd mony a weary foot,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But we've wandered many a weary foot,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">Sin' auld lang syne.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Since old times long ago.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">We twa hae paidled in the burn,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We two have paddled in the stream,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">Frae mornin' sun till dine;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From morning sun till dinner-time;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">But seas between us braid hae roar'd,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But broad seas have roared between us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">Sin' auld lang syne.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Since old times long ago.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">And here's a hand, my trusty frien',</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And here's a hand, my trusty friend,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">And gie's a hand o' thine;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And give me a hand of yours;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">And we'll tak' a right guid willie-waught,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we'll take a right good drink of goodwill,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">For auld lang syne.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For old times long ago.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">And surely ye'll be your pint-stoup,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And surely you'll pay for your pint-cup,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">And surely I'll be mine;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And surely I'll pay for mine;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">And we'll tak' a cup o' kindness yet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we'll take a cup of kindness yet,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sco">For auld lang syne.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For old times long ago.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Robert Burns (1788). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Burns'_Popular_Songs%2FAuld_Lang_Syne" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>'auld lang syne' is literally 'old long since': the old days, times long past. It is rendered 'old times long ago' throughout, and 'days o' lang syne' (1.4) as 'the days of long ago'.</li><li>line 3.1: 'braes' are hillsides or slopes, often by a river.</li><li>line 3.2: 'gowans' are daisies (or other wild field flowers); 'fine' is pretty, fine.</li><li>line 3.3: 'mony a weary foot' is literally 'many a weary foot': many a tiring step or mile; kept as written.</li><li>line 4.2: 'dine' is dinner, the midday meal in Burns's Scotland: from morning till midday.</li><li>line 4.3: 'braid' is broad.</li><li>line 5.2: 'gie's' is 'give us', in Scots usage 'give me'.</li><li>line 5.3: 'willie-waught' is a hearty drink taken together; the word comes from a misdivision of 'gude-willie waught' (a good-will draught), so its exact sense is debated.</li><li>line 6.1-2: 'ye'll be your pint-stoup' means 'you will stand for (pay for) your own tankard'; a 'stoup' is a drinking vessel. The sense 'pay for' is the usual reading, not a word-for-word one.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
