<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song of the Old Folks (Albert Laighton)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1855</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Plenary (Sacred Harp)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune, a hymn text</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fair Haven (hymn, words by Amos Sutton)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">You're a Grand Old Flag (George M. Cohan)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company (John Philip Sousa)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1924</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">We're Here Because We're Here</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">First World War</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune, soldiers' song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ce n'est qu'un au revoir (Jacques Sevin)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1920</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Restons toujours unis</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune, Catholic hymn</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Skuld gammel venskab rejn forgo (Jeppe Aakjær)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1927</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation into the Salling dialect of Danish</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Purano shei diner kotha (Rabindranath Tagore)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">inspired by the melody</span></li><li><a href="/songs/hotaru-no-hikari">Hotaru no Hikari</a><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune, Japanese words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jakbyeol / anthem of the Korean exile government and of South Korea</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1919–1948</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Old Long Syne (printed by James Watson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1711</span><span class="sg-chain-h">an earlier ballad from the same old song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">An older Scottish folk song</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the song Burns took down</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Miller's Wedding (strathspey)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">possible common source of the tune</span></li></ul></section>
